From cb7d866d9259728a18fab01085925c2889fcfd5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Medium1992 <Medium1992@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 08:47:58 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] XHTTP: Do not cut a server's downloads against a queue that
 other traffic keeps

A server cut download caps against any queue the kernel saw. A queue that other traffic keeps full does not shorten when it does: pi took a download to init per round trip, about 1 Mbit/s with a second of other traffic in the queue. With four other flows on a 60 Mbit/s link at RTT 144 ms for 20 seconds, a download fell from 56 to 0-1 Mbit/s for most of them and climbed back in steps after; without the governor it kept 34 on average.

Once the queue stands at twice a quarter of the empty path, the server watches what its downloads to the client have in flight and what its connections have sent and not had acknowledged. When both have come down to half of the most they were and the queue has not come down by a quarter for two round trips, the queue is foreign: the downloads are neither cut nor held against it, as without a governor, until it stands drained for two round trips or 32 round trips pass. It is judged per client address, against the least round trip of its connections, and the state outlives them for five minutes.

The same burst now averages 20 Mbit/s and recovers at once; beside one direct flow at RTT 150 ms a download gets 1.8-3.6 Mbit/s where it got 1.1.
